General elections were held in [[Dominican Republic]] on 15 May 2016 to elect a president, vice-president and the [[National Congress of the Dominican Republic|Congress]], as well as 20 deputies to the [[Central American Parliament]], municipal councils, mayors and vice mayors.<ref name=IFES>[http://www.ifes.org/~/media/Files/Publications/White%20PaperReport/2012/Elections_in_the_Dominican_Republic_2012.pdf Elections  in  the  Dominican  Republic : May  20  Presidential Election] {{webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20141031030856/http://www.ifes.org/~/media/Files/Publications/White%20PaperReport/2012/Elections_in_the_Dominican_Republic_2012.pdf |date=October 31, 2014 }} IFES</ref><ref>[http://dr1.com/premium/news/2013/dnews091313.shtml Daily News] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160514030840/http://dr1.com/premium/news/2013/dnews091313.shtml |date=May 14, 2016 }} DR1, 13 September 2013</ref> On 15 May 2015 Roberto Rosario, president of the [[Central Electoral Board]], said that there would be about 4,300 seats up for election in the "most complex elections in history".<ref>[http://www.elcaribe.com.do/2015/05/15/falta-ano-para-las-elecciones-mas-complejas Falta un año para las elecciones más complejas] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150518032156/http://www.elcaribe.com.do/2015/05/15/falta-ano-para-las-elecciones-mas-complejas |date=May 18, 2015 }} elCaribe, 15 May 2015 {{in lang|es}}</ref>

==Background==
The previous [[2010 Dominican Republic parliamentary election|parliamentary elections]] were held in 2010, and fresh elections would have usually been due in 2014 as [[Congress of the Dominican Republic|Congress]] has a four-year term. However, in an effort to revert to the pre-1996 system and synchronize the dates of presidential and parliamentary and local elections in a single electoral year, the congressional term starting in 2010 was exceptionally extended to six years in order for the next congressional and municipal elections to be held alongside the next presidential elections due in 2016.

On 19 April 2015 the political committee of the ruling [[Dominican Liberation Party]] decided, without consensus, to amend the constitution to allow a president to be re-elected once, allowing incumbent President [[Danilo Medina]] to be presented for re-election, based on his high poll ratings.<ref>[http://www.diariolibre.com/noticias/2015/04/20/i1107661_pld-someter-reforma-constitucin-para-reeleccin.html PLD someterá reforma a la Constitución para la reelección] Diario Libre, 20 April 2015 {{in lang|es}}</ref> This led to tensions between party members and leaders, especially amongst supporters of [[Leonel Fernandez]] who was a pre-candidate for the elections. There was also an impasse in the [[Senate of the Dominican Republic|Senate]] and [[Chamber of Deputies of the Dominican Republic|Chamber of Deputies]], where senators and representatives close to Fernandez stated that they would not vote in favour of the Act to Call the Revising National Assembly to amend the constitution.<ref>[http://elnacional.com.do/diputados-del-pld-rechazan-modificacion-de-la-constitucion/ Diputados de Leonel se rebelan contra CP del PLD] El Nacional, 20 April 2015 {{in lang|es}}</ref><ref>[http://www.santiagoinforma.com.do/noticias/nacionales/item/1238-senadores-del-sector-leonel-dicen-ser%C3%ADa-incoherente-restablecer-reelecci%C3%B3n Senadores del sector leonel dicen sería "incoherente" restablecer reelección] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20151003010645/http://www.santiagoinforma.com.do/noticias/nacionales/item/1238-senadores-del-sector-leonel-dicen-ser%C3%ADa-incoherente-restablecer-reelecci%C3%B3n |date=October 3, 2015 }} Santiago Informa {{in lang|es}}</ref><ref>[http://www.diariolibre.com/noticias/2015/04/21/i1110311_legisladores-peledestas-desacuerdo-con-lnea-del-comit-poltico.html Legisladores peledeístas en desacuerdo con línea del Comité Político] DiarioLibre, 21 April 2015 {{in lang|es}}</ref> After several weeks of internal disputes, the political committee, including Fernandez and Medina, agreed on 28 May to vote for the amendment.<ref>[http://www.listindiario.com/la-republica/2015/05/28/368872/pld-presenta-acuerdo-entre-danilo-medina-y-leonel-fernndez PLD presenta acuerdo entre Danilo Medina y Leonel Fernández] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160305011338/http://www.listindiario.com/la-republica/2015/05/28/368872/pld-presenta-acuerdo-entre-danilo-medina-y-leonel-fernndez |date=March 5, 2016 }} Listin Diario, 28 May 2015 {{in lang|es}}</ref> The amendment passed the Chamber on June 2.<ref>{{cite news | accessdate= 9 April 2016 | publisher = Bloomberg News | first = Ezra | last = Fieser | title = Dominican Republic Approves Law Allowing Medina to Run in 2016 | url = https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2015-06-02/dominican-republic-approves-law-allowing-medina-to-run-in-2016 | date = 2 June 2016}}</ref>

==Electoral system==
The president was elected using the [[two-round system]]; if no candidate had received more than 50% plus 1 of the vote, a second-round [[Two-round system|runoff]] would have been held in June 2016. Presidents are limited to serving two consecutive terms of four years.

In the Congress, the 190 members of the [[Chamber of Deputies of the Dominican Republic|Chamber of Deputies]] were elected in three groups; 178 were elected using proportional representation from 32 multi-member constituencies based on the 31 [[provinces of the Dominican Republic|provinces]] and the [[Distrito Nacional]], with the number of seats based on the population of each province. A further seven were elected by proportional representation in a separate constituency for expatriates and five allocated nationally to parties that received at least 1% of the vote, with preference given to those that did not win any of the 178 constituency seats.<ref name=IPU>[http://www.ipu.org/parline-e/reports/2093_B.htm Cámara de Diputados (Chamber of Deputies)] IPU</ref> The 32 members of the [[Senate of the Dominican Republic|Senate]] were elected from the 31 provinces and the Distrito Nacional using [[first-past-the-post voting]].<ref>[http://www.ipu.org/parline-e/reports/2094_B.htm Senado (Senate)] IPU</ref>

